Below are suggested questions you can use or include in an email. Tell me which items to include or if you want me to draft/send the message.

Questions about atrial fibrillation management
- Do I currently have AFib (paroxysmal, persistent, or permanent)? If so, what is the recommended monitoring plan (EKG, Holter, event monitor)?  
- Based on my history and risk factors, do I need anticoagulation now or is it safe to wait? (I’m happy to provide a current list of meds and any recent test results.)  
- If anticoagulation is recommended, what options do you suggest and what are the risks/benefits given my age and other conditions?  
- Do any of my current meds (omeprazole, lisinopril, atorvastatin) interact with anticoagulants or affect management?  
- Are there lifestyle or blood-pressure targets you want me to follow to reduce AFib risk/episodes?  
- Should I be referred to a cardiologist or electrophysiologist for AFib management or ablation consideration?

Questions about orthopedics / referral to Northwest Orthopedics
- Given my age and history of back pain, do you recommend an orthopedic referral or imaging (X-ray, MRI)?  
- Are there red-flag symptoms that would make a referral urgent (leg weakness, changes in bowel/bladder, progressive numbness)?  
- Would conservative management (physical therapy, injections, pain clinic) be appropriate first-line? If so, should you refer me to PT or a pain specialist?  
- If an orthopedic consult is recommended, can you refer me to Northwest Orthopedics (or another you prefer)?

Items to bring up at the annual physical / tests to consider
- Review blood pressure control and current meds (lisinopril 20 mg). Consider home BP log or ambulatory monitoring.  
- Update labs: CBC (for anemia), CMP, lipid panel, A1C (you mentioned a prior A1C — do you mean 3.20% or a different value?), thyroid function, and any cardiac markers as you think appropriate.  
- Medication reconciliation and discussion of side effects or interactions.  
- Vaccinations, bone health screening (DEXA if indicated), and fall-risk assessment given age and back issues.

Next steps I can take for you
- Draft an email to Dr. Amy Foster including these questions (you can review before sending).  
- Search your inbox for recent messages from Dr. Foster or Northwest Orthopedics.  
- Create a printable one-page question list for your appointment.  

Which would you like me to do? If drafting/sending, tell me whether to send now or save as a draft and any tone or extra details to include.

